    <!-- Touch/keyboard haptic durations for the coin DC motor, in MILLISECONDS. STOCK VALUES (1.2.2).
         On this hardware Samsung reinterprets "magnitude" as a pulse length:
         VibrationSettings.updateCustomSettings() does
             mTouchMagnitude = LEVEL_TO_TOUCH_MAGNITUDE[Settings.System VIB_FEEDBACK_MAGNITUDE]
         and SemDcVibration.getVibration() then does createOneShot(mMagnitude, DEFAULT_AMPLITUDE).
         So every index below is literally "how many ms the motor spins", and the Settings touch
         intensity slider picks the index. It retunes Samsung Keyboard keypresses, long press, the
         navigation buttons, gestures and every other DC click (SEP 50124) at once.
         A new install starts at index 1, not 3: the 8.5 SettingsProvider writes
         VIB_FEEDBACK_MAGNITUDE=def_vibration_intensity_on_dc (1) and turns keyboard vibration off on
         a DC device, as stock T970 does (ops/release122/haptic_audit/VIBRATION_AUDIT.md 1.6).
         History: 1.0-1.2.1 shipped 0/40/60/90 (90 picked by the owner for strength, 07-28). On
         10-10 the owner chose stock 0/45/55/70 and the slider to adjust it: at 90 the motor ran 97 %
         of a fast typing burst and every key smeared into one buzz (70: 88 %, 45: 61 %), and stock
         T970 and the X810 donor both use these values. 45 is above the ~40 ms perception floor
         (handoff/04 SS1.1). The owner felt 45 and 70 on the T970 before choosing (10-10 22:43).
         Needs at least 3 entries (updateCustomSettings skips the block when length-1 < 2); 4 is
         right because the slider has 4 stops on a DC motor.
         A value ABOVE length-1 is not clamped - updateCustomSettings writes it through as raw
         milliseconds. A NEGATIVE one throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException out of the settings
         observer callback, uncaught, and the bad value persists in the settings DB. -->
    <integer-array name="config_valuesOfDcMotorDefaultTouchMagnitudeLevel">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>45</item>
        <item>55</item>
        <item>70</item>
    </integer-array>
    <!-- Samsung SEP pattern table, as played on a DC motor. VibratorManagerService.onCustomSystemReady
         loads SEP index n (constant 50024 + n) from config_vibeCommon*, and with no haptic engine
         SemPatternVibration plays the array as createWaveform(pattern, -1): plain IVibrator::on()
         pulses. Donor X810 and stock T970 both ship A=[0,7] and E=[0,10,60,15] - 7/10/15 ms pulses
         that reach the motor and are felt as nothing here (handoff/04 SS1.1: floor ~40 ms; the X810
         drives the same 7 ms through sec_vibrator overdrive, msm_vibrator here has none).
           A (index 1) is global: every vibrate() of semCreateHaptic(50025) or
             semGetVibrationIndex(1) in any app plays it - S Pen detach
             (PhoneWindowManagerExt.notifyPenSwitchChanged, measured "for 7ms" on a tester log),
             AOD volume panel, camera scan, message swipe, and (not traced) the Settings intensity preview.
           E (index 5): S Pen attach (same method, CscFeature_Common_SupportSpenEditionVibration off)
         A goes to 40 ms (owner, 2026-10-02): the shortest pulse this motor reliably shows, so the
         apps that call it in quick succession stay crisp. E stays two pulses at the measured
         40 + 60 gap + 40, which handoff/04 SS1.1 recorded as two distinct bumps on this motor.
         B, F, N, O and the _22.._114 entries still carry 7 ms; only A and E are user-facing here. -->
    <integer-array name="config_vibeCommonA">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>40</item>
    </integer-array>
    <integer-array name="config_vibeCommonE">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>40</item>
        <item>60</item>
        <item>40</item>
    </integer-array>
    <!-- The X810 8.5 donor force-enables ANGLE (GLES-on-Vulkan) for 13 games including
         Minecraft. This vendor is Android 13: its Vulkan driver reports 1.1.128, far older
         than anything that allowlist was validated on, and AOSP itself gates ANGLE-for-games
         by ro.vendor.api_level while the explicit allowlist skips that gate. The 8.0 donor
         shipped this list empty and its games rendered clean. Empty it again so games get
         the native Adreno GLES driver. -->
    <string-array name="config_angleAllowList" />
